course: dictionary
lesson: 11236
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Working» is a good example. The words around it show you which meaning the writer wants, so read the clues before you choose.
explain: «Most home fire deaths happen in homes without a working alarm.» Here working means in good order, not broken. An alarm that works is one that can warn you. «Isaac Newton built an early working reflecting telescope in 1668.» Here it means a telescope that really worked, so it could be used to look at the sky.
explain: «A teacher can spot copied answers: every answer is right, but there is no working.» Here working means the steps written down that show how you got your answer. Try your meaning in the sentence: «there are no steps written down» makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Working can be a verb, an adjective or a noun, depending on the sentence.
fact: As an adjective, «working» means in good order, not broken: a working alarm.
fact: As a noun, «the working» means the steps written down, as in «show your working».
fact: «Working hours» are the hours of a job, and «working people» are people who have jobs.
fact: «Working» can also mean a model that really moves or runs.
exercise: «Most home fire deaths happen in homes without a working alarm.» What does working mean here? | *In good order, not broken | Very noisy | Doing a job for money | Written down step by step
exercise: A teacher says, «Show your working» in a maths test. What does she want to see? | Your name and the date | A drawing of a machine | Your favourite sum | *The steps you wrote down to get your answer
exercise: Find and copy the one word that tells you the dog is doing its job, from «never distract a working guide dog.» | never | distract | *working | guide
test: What is a working machine? | *One that is not broken and can run | Very heavy and slow | Broken and rusty | Painted bright red
test: Priya's mum is one of many working parents. What does working mean here? | Playing games with children | *Having a paid job | Written down step by step | Stuck and not moving
exam: In «the lift is working», working means: | Stuck between two floors | Doing a job in an office | *Not broken, doing what it should | Painted and clean
